ly one hole. Since each rectangle covers two rows each of two columns, we need to ensure that each row is covered by exactly one rectangle (or part of a rectangle) such that after removal we have exactly one uncovered per row.\n\nIf we have three 2x2 rectangles, they cover 6 rows (counting multiplicity), but there are only 4 rows. So some rows must be covered by more than one rectangle (i.e., overlapping rows). But rectangles cannot overlap. So a row can be part of up to two rectangles (if they occupy disjoint columns). For a given row, we could have two 2x2 rectangles covering different columns. That would occupy the entire row (4 squares) leaving no hole. That's not allowed because each row must have exactly one uncovered square. So each row must contain exactly 3 covered squares (since one uncovered). But a 2x2 rectangle covering a row contributes 2 squares to that row. So each row must be covered by either one rectangle (covering 2 squares) plus another rectangle covering 1 square? But rectangles have integer dimensions, cannot cover 1 square unless it's a 1x1 rectangle. So we could have some 1x1 rectangles.\n\nThus the decomposition may involve 1x1 rectangles as holes? Actually holes are uncovered squares. Tiles are rectangles covering at least one square. Could be 1x1 tile (a unit square). The problem didn't forbid tiles being size 1x1? It said rectangular tiles of possibly different sizes; a unit square is a rectangle. So we can use 1x1 tiles. So we can have a tiling with many 1x1 tiles. But we want to minimize number of tiles, so we want larger rectangles.\n\nThus for n=4, maybe we can tile with 3 rectangles, one of size 2x3 (cover 6 squares) and two of size 3x1 (cover 3 each) total 12 squares. Let's attempt to find such rectangles.\n\nGoal: find three rectangles A (size 2x3), B (size 3x1), C (size 3x1), covering all free cells, leaving holes as permutation.\n\nLet's denote rows 1..4, columns 1..4. We need to leave holes (i, pi(i)). Let's try to construct.\n