e column offset (from 0 to k-1). The left part of that block is a rectangle of width equal to the column offset of the hole within the block. So across blocks, the left part widths differ. So the union of left parts across blocks is not a rectangle; it's a shape with steps. However, we can combine them into at most k rectangles per strip: each column of blocks contributes a vertical rectangle. But maybe we can combine left parts of all blocks in a strip into at most k rectangles: one for each column group of blocks? That's too many.\n\nBut perhaps we can choose the placement of holes within each block such that the column offset is the same across all blocks in a horizontal strip. If we arrange holes such that in each row group, the holes are all at the same column offset within their block (i.e., each block's hole column offset equals the block's column index). That is exactly the \"grid permutation\" described earlier: within block (a,b), the hole is at offset (b,a). So the column offset within block (a,b) is a (the row group index). For a fixed horizontal strip (i.e., fixed row group a), across blocks (a,b) as b varies, the hole column offset is a (constant). So indeed, within each horizontal strip, all holes are at the same column offset (equal to the strip index a). This means that within each block in that strip, the left part width (i.e., columns left of the hole within the block) is exactly a. So the left parts of all blocks in that strip have the same width a, and they are contiguous across blocks because each block is adjacent horizontally. Therefore, the union of left parts across all blocks in the strip forms a rectangle: rows = the k rows of the strip, columns = the first a columns of each block across all blocks, which corresponds to the first a columns of each block across the entire strip, i.e., a * k columns total (since each block contributes a columns). So left part region across the strip is a rectangle of size k rows by a*k columns. Similarly, t